<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of London (1867)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1867</span> · Also known as Second Treaty of London</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of London of 11 May 1867 settled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg-crisis">Luxembourg Crisis</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. Signed by the great powers, it declared the Grand Duchy of Luxembourg perpetually neutral under their collective guarantee, confirmed its union with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-orange-nassau">House of Orange-Nassau</a>, required the withdrawal of the Prussian garrison and ordered the demolition of the fortress. It is regarded as a foundation of Luxembourg's independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1867.jpg" alt="Treaty of London (1867)" width="320" height="253" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Archives nationales du Luxembourg. Published under licence PD-0 - Public domain. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Treaty_of_London_1867_Art_VII_and_signatures.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
